+# OUTLAW CITY: N4 street-sign rollout readiness (PLAN ONLY)
+
+**Status:** PLAN AWAITING ARCHITECT REVIEW. Nothing was changed in Studio, and no sign was built. Prepared 2026-10-16 from `CityLayout.luau` and one read-only Studio query against place 132163592957463 (exact output in `docs/audits/2026-10-16_n4-rollout-readiness/TEST_EVIDENCE.md`).
+
+**Starting point:**
+- One approved sign: `StreetSign_100_100` (Architect APPROVE, `dc32b76`), at corner (122, 122), cantilevered, "3rd St" / "C Ave". It stays unchanged.
+- **Full rollout is not authorized.** This plan asks which further signs, in which order, with which gates.
+
+**Boundaries:**
+- No new roads, buildings, districts or gameplay.
+- No changes to police (pursuit 130 / 100 / 0.25 / 8), vehicle physics, robbery, economy, dealership, jail or anti-cheat (monitor-only).
+- Siren off; one police unit; no external assets.
+- Multiplayer is MULTIPLAYER UNVERIFIED.
+
+## 1. Method
+For each of the 16 grid junctions (X, Z ∈ {−300, −100, 100, 300}) and each of its 4 corners, I applied the approved design unchanged:
+- **Post:** at (X ± 22, Z ± 22).
+- **Blades:** each 7 × 1.5 × 0.2, cantilevered from the post face away from the junction (`CityLayout.streetSigns()` already derives the direction from the corner's sign).
+
+Then I checked four things.
+
+1. **Footprints.** Each of the 3 parts' full rectangle must lie on one `Sidewalks` part. I measured the gaps to roads, the highway and the plaza; to every marking (stop lines and centre lines); to buildings, the store, the dealership, the jail and barriers (at least 3 studs required); and to vehicle and civilian spawns.
+2. **Arms.** Which road arms enter the junction (a road continues past the square). This matches the 50 approaches recorded for N5.
+3. **Sightlines (a computed screen, not a render).** A driver reads the **cross-street** blade:
+   - driving along an avenue (N/S), the driver reads the street blade (`Blade_EW`, faces ±Z);
+   - driving along a street (E/W), the driver reads the avenue blade (`Blade_NS`, faces ±X).
+
+   For each incoming approach I placed a driver eye at y = 5 in the incoming (right-hand) lane, 30, 45, 60 and 75 studs before the junction centre. A point counts if all three hold:
+   - the blade is within 40° of the driving direction;
+   - its face is turned at least 0.3 towards the driver;
+   - a ray from the eye to the blade hits nothing (buildings and the store block it).
+
+   The score is the number of passing distances out of 4.
+4. **Cross-check against recorded captures.** For the existing (100, 100) sign the screen gives N 4/4, S 3/4, W 4/4, E 1/4. This matches the approved captures: readable from the north, south and west; out of view from the east stop line.
+
+## 2. Results for all 64 corners
+**31 corners fit, 33 are rejected:**
+- **28 off-grid:** the corner is outside the outer roads, with no sidewalk.
+- **4 on the plaza:** the four corners facing the central plaza; the plaza is a drivable surface, not a sidewalk.
+- **1 overlapping the 24/7 MART** at (−100, −100) corner (−, +): it overlaps `StoreWallNorth` and is blocked from three approaches.
+
+**Every fitting corner has:**
+- post face 3.80 and blades 3.90 from the nearest road edge (the same as the approved sign);
+- nearest marking at least 4.84;
+- no vehicle or civilian spawn within 36 studs.
+
+The nearest solid object varies; the smallest is 6.40 studs (Building_3_2_3), all above the 3-stud minimum.
+
+**Sightline pattern** (the important finding):
+- With the cantilever design, one sign at a 4-arm junction serves **3 of 4 approaches** well (3–4 of 4 points).
+- The 4th approach is the lane that passes right beside the corner: the sign is far to the side until about 75 studs out (1 of 4).
+- A 3-arm junction gets 2 of 3 approaches; a 2-arm corner gets 1 of 2.
+
+So each junction has one weak approach, and the choice of corner decides **which** approach is weak.
+
+**Corner rule** (consistent with the approved sign): choose the corner whose weak approach comes from the **outer edge** of the grid, or along the perimeter road. Interior and destination traffic is then always served.
+
+**Do cantilever directions need to vary?** No new rule is needed. "Away from the junction" already flips per corner, and every chosen corner below keeps both blades on its sidewalk block.
+
+## 3. Ranked candidates (one sign per junction, best corner)
+Scores are approaches served (passing distances out of 4). "Nearest solid" is the closest building, store, dealership or jail part to any of the 3 sign parts.
