+
+**Notes on ranking:**
+- Ties between valid corners at the same junction were broken by the corner rule.
+- Rank 5's weak approach is the 200-stud link from the other connector junction, which has just been signed.
+- At (−100, 100) and (100, −100) a third corner is also valid; the chosen one follows the rule.
+
+**Where the current design does not work well without changes:**
+- **The four 2-arm corners (rank 12–15).** Only one corner has a sidewalk, and it serves only one of the two approaches. A better sign would need a sidewalk outside the grid (none exists) or a different design. **Not recommended.**
+- **(−100, −100) corner (−, +)** is unusable: it hits the store. The chosen corner (−, −) avoids it.
+- **The weak approach at every junction.** If every approach must read a sign, a junction needs a **second sign on the diagonally opposite corner**. That doubles the parts, and on most junctions the opposite corner is the plaza or off-grid. Not proposed.
+
